## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic synchronization primitives and common OS patterns, especially producer-consumer, deadlock avoidance, and correct semaphore/mutex usage in C.

## Key Concepts
- Semaphores
- Mutex
- Circular buffer
- Deadlock detection
- Boundary conditions (full/empty)

## Code Example
```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define N 10

int buffer[N];
int in = 0, out = 0;
sem_t empty, full;
pthread_mutex_t mutex;

void produce(int item) {
  sem_wait(&empty);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  buffer[in] = item;
  in = (in + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&full);
}

int consume() {
  int item;
  sem_wait(&full);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  item = buffer[out];
  out = (out + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&empty);
  return item;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers and ensure fairness?
- What are trade-offs vs. using condition variables instead of semaphores?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of memory ordering, lock-free data structures, and weak memory models in OS CPUs. It probes ability to identify a real race and reason about inter-thread visibility across architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free synchronization
- memory_order_release
- memory_order_acquire
- Data visibility before signaling availability

## Code Example

```cpp
// Pseudo-C++ lock-free sketch
std::atomic<size_t> head{0}, tail{0};
int data[N];

void produce(int v){
  auto t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  data[t % N] = v;
  tail.store((t+1) % N, std::memory_order_release);
}

int consume(){
  auto t = tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  auto h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  if (h == t) return -1;
  int v = data[h % N];
  head.store((h+1) % N, std::memory_order_release);
  return v;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this pattern for multiple producers/consumers?
- What tests would you add to validate correctness across ARM/x86?

